  <h2 id="short-answer">The short answer to "is merchant navy a good career"</h2>

  <p>
    Merchant navy remains a genuinely strong career in India in 2026 for the right person, but the honest answer
    depends far more on lifestyle fit than most recruitment marketing admits. Pay is genuinely strong and
    tax-advantaged. The trade-off is real, sustained separation from family that recruitment brochures tend to
    underweight in favour of the pay and travel appeal.
  </p>

  <p>
    The most useful reframe: this is not primarily a pay decision. Most people considering merchant navy already
    know the pay is competitive. The decision that actually determines whether the career fits is whether 4-9
    month continuous sea contracts, with limited connectivity, are genuinely sustainable for you and your family.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      This is not the "see the world, earn tax-free income" pitch some maritime academy marketing leans on, and it
      is not a warning to avoid the field either. Both miss the real picture.
    </p>
    <p>
      The pay genuinely is strong, and the travel genuinely is real. The separation from family is also genuinely
      difficult for many officers, and is the actual reason most eventually move ashore — not because the job
      itself was disappointing. Both things are true at once, and the honest decision depends on which trade-off
      you are genuinely prepared to accept.
    </p>
  </div>

